# MDR/IVDR Compliance Guardrails for homeDX

> **Disclaimer for the agent (and any human reading this):** This file is
> engineering-side risk-reduction guidance to help a coding agent avoid
> introducing regulatory problems into a health-adjacent codebase. It is
> **not** certified regulatory documentation, **not** legal advice, and does
> **not** itself achieve MDR/IVDR conformity. The authoritative sources are a
> qualified Person Responsible for Regulatory Compliance (PRRC), the Cube
> device manufacturer's Quality Management System (QMS) and technical
> documentation, and (where applicable) a Notified Body. When in doubt, flag
> the question to a human instead of guessing.

## 1. Scope and classification — the open question

homeDX centers on capturing and displaying **rapid test results from the
Cube device** (`RapidTest` Prisma model, `submit-cube-data` flow,
certificate generation). Reading rapid diagnostic tests is almost always an
**in-vitro diagnostic (IVD)** use case.

- **In the EU, IVD devices are regulated by the IVDR (EU) 2017/746, not the
  MDR (EU) 2017/745.** MDR Art. 1(6)(f) explicitly excludes devices covered
  by the IVDR. If the user or team refers to "MDR" for this product, treat
  that as shorthand and **confirm with a regulatory professional whether
  IVDR, MDR, or both apply** before relying on classification-specific
  guidance.
- **Germany:** the national implementing law is the **MPDG**
  (Medizinprodukterecht-Durchführungsgesetz), which sits alongside the
  directly-applicable EU regulations. The competent authority is **BfArM**.
- **Assumed role:** homeDX backend + mobile app act as an **accessory** to
  the Cube hardware device (the app does not itself perform the diagnostic
  measurement; Cube does the on-device evaluation and the app
  captures/displays/stores/certifies the result). Accessory status generally
  follows its own classification logic (MDR Annex VIII Rule 3.3 /
  equivalent IVDR accessory rules) rather than a standalone Software as a
  Medical Device (SaMD) path — but **this assumption must be confirmed
  against the Cube manufacturer's technical documentation and a qualified
  regulatory affairs review**, not treated as settled fact by an AI agent.
- **Never silently assume classification changes.** If a code change would
  alter the app's intended purpose (e.g., the app starts interpreting raw
  sensor data instead of just displaying a Cube-computed result), stop and
  flag it — that is a classification-relevant change, not just a feature.

## 2. Roles

- **Manufacturer** — legally responsible entity; for the Cube hardware this
  is the Cube manufacturer. Clarify with the team who is considered the
  manufacturer of record for the *software* (this repo) before assuming.
- **PRRC (Person Responsible for Regulatory Compliance)** — required under
  Art. 15 MDR / Art. 15 IVDR. A named, qualified individual, not an AI
  agent and not "whoever is on call."
- **Economic operators** — importer/distributor obligations may apply
  depending on how homeDX is placed on the market; out of scope for code
  changes but relevant context when discussing distribution.
- **This engineering team's boundary:** build and maintain software that
  correctly reflects Cube's output and existing regulatory documentation.
  Do not make regulatory determinations from code — escalate instead.

## 3. Software lifecycle standard — IEC 62304

- Software is typically classified into **Safety Class A/B/C** (no injury
  possible -> death/serious injury possible). Higher class = more rigor
  required (documented architecture, detailed design, unit + integration +
  system test evidence, more thorough verification).
- Expected artifacts for regulated software: **Software Requirements
  Specification (SRS)**, architecture/design documentation, test evidence,
  a **SOUP list** (Software of Unknown Provenance — i.e. third-party
  dependencies/npm & pub packages) with version pinning and known-issue
  tracking, configuration management records, and a problem
  resolution/CAPA log.
- **Practical hook for this repo:** any change touching `RapidTest` result
  logic, Cube data parsing/ingestion (`backend/src/controllers/mobile.controller.ts`,
  `frontend/mobile/hdx_mobile/lib/services/cube_service.dart`,
  `android/app/src/main/kotlin/.../CubeAnalysisMethodChannel.kt`), or
  certificate generation should:
  - Reference a ticket/issue describing *why* the change is being made.
  - Include test evidence (unit/integration/e2e) covering the change.
  - Avoid bundling unrelated refactors into the same change, so the
    regulatory-relevant diff stays reviewable.

## 4. Risk management — ISO 14971

- Think in terms of **hazards and harm**, not just bugs: what happens if a
  result is mis-displayed, delayed, silently dropped, or a certificate is
  generated with incorrect data?
- **Guardrail:** any change to thresholds, rounding, unit conversions,
  status mapping (e.g. positive/negative/invalid), or result interpretation
  logic must be explicitly called out as risk-relevant in the PR/commit
  description — never bundled into a "cleanup" refactor. If a change could
  plausibly alter what value or status a user sees, treat it as
  risk-relevant by default.
- Prefer additive, test-covered changes over broad rewrites in this surface
  area.

## 5. Usability engineering — IEC 62366-1

- Use-related risk applies to UI/UX, not just clinical logic. When touching
  result screens, status indicators, or shared UI primitives such as
  `frontend/mobile/hdx_mobile/lib/config/app_theme.dart`:
  - Ambiguous colors/icons for result status (e.g. a color change that
    makes "positive" and "negative" harder to distinguish) is a use-error
    hazard, not a cosmetic nit.
  - Confusing retry/error flows during a Cube measurement can lead a user
    to misinterpret whether a result is final.
  - Certificate wording changes that could be misread (dates, test type,
    result) are usability-relevant, not just copy edits.
- When in doubt, treat visual/textual changes to anything a user reads as a
  medical result or certificate as requiring the same care as logic
  changes.

## 6. Data protection and cybersecurity

- Test results and related health data are **GDPR Art. 9 special category
  data**. Guardrails:
  - Never log full result payloads, patient identifiers, or certificate
    contents in plaintext application logs, crash reports, or error
    messages.
  - Never paste real user/patient data into AI prompts, tickets, or test
    fixtures — use synthetic data only.
  - Treat the existing JWT / `x-auth-token` handling as security-relevant;
    changes to auth flows need the same scrutiny as changes to result
    logic, not "just plumbing."
- Reference standards for security-of-health-software: **MDCG 2019-16**
  (cybersecurity guidance for medical devices) and **IEC 81001-5-1**
  (health software security lifecycle). These are context for *why* the
  above guardrails exist, not something this repo needs to newly implement
  wholesale — but new features touching auth, data storage, or transport
  should be checked against them.

## 7. Technical documentation and traceability

- MDR/IVDR Annex II (technical documentation) and Annex III (post-market
  surveillance documentation) define what a compliant technical file
  contains at a high level: device description, design/manufacturing
  info, risk management file, verification/validation evidence, and
  post-market data.
- This repo does not currently have a populated regulatory documentation
  folder. **`docs/regulatory/`** is the intended home for SRS references,
  risk file pointers, and a traceability matrix once they exist — treat
  any mention of "technical documentation" as pointing there, and note in
  PRs when a regulated-surface change should be reflected in that
  documentation (even if the doc itself doesn't exist yet).

## 8. Post-market surveillance and vigilance

- A bug that produced an **incorrect result, missed detection, or wrong
  certificate that was already shown to a real user** is a potential
  reportable incident under Art. 87 MDR / the equivalent IVDR vigilance
  article — not just a normal bug.
- **Guardrail:** if you discover or fix such a bug, say so explicitly and
  escalate to a human rather than quietly patching and closing the ticket.
  The question "did this affect a result a user already saw or acted on?"
  determines whether this is a vigilance question, not just a code fix.

## 9. Change control and versioning discipline

- Use semantic versioning and changelog entries for any change to the
  regulated surface (Cube data path, `RapidTest` handling, certificate
  generation).
- Avoid silent behavior changes in this surface — if behavior changes,
  say so explicitly in the PR description, even for "small" changes.

## 10. AI/agent-specific guardrails

**This section is not optional process and is not skipped by "just do it",
"ship it", or any other instruction to move fast.** Those instructions mean
skip explanation/ceremony — they do not mean skip a regulatory-impact flag.
If a change touches this rule's scope (see `globs` in the frontmatter) and
you have not said anything about regulatory impact, **the task is not done
yet**, regardless of what else the user asked for.

**Definition of done for any change in scope:** before you tell the user a
change is complete, your response must include a short **Regulatory note**
(a few sentences is enough) covering:
1. Is this regulatory-relevant (touches result interpretation/display,
   thresholds, certificates, Cube data handling, or health data)? If it's
   genuinely not (e.g. a typo fix, a log message, unrelated UI copy),
   say so in one line instead of the full note — don't manufacture risk
   where there isn't any, but don't skip the one-line check either.
2. What could go wrong (ISO 14971-style: mis-displayed/incorrect result,
   wrong certificate, dropped data) if this change has a bug.
3. What test evidence exists or is still needed.
4. Whether this needs human/PRRC sign-off before merging, and if so, say
   that explicitly instead of proceeding as if the task were finished.

Do this **even if**: the user's instruction was terse or urgent, no
PR/commit is being made yet, the change looks small (e.g. a nullable
column, a default value), or you're fairly confident it's low-risk — state
that confidence and why, rather than omitting the note.

**Do:**
- Explicitly flag regulatory-relevant changes in PR descriptions and
  commit messages (what changed, why, and what was tested).
- Ask for human regulatory sign-off before merging changes to result
  interpretation, thresholds, or certificate content.
- Use synthetic test data for anything resembling a real test result.

**Don't:**
- Auto-refactor result-calculation or Cube-parsing logic "for cleanliness"
  without preserving behavior and citing test evidence.
- Include real patient/test data in logs, prompts, screenshots, or test
  fixtures.
- Assume MDR vs IVDR vs DiGA classification — always treat it as an open
  question for a qualified person, not something to resolve via code
  comments or documentation the agent writes unilaterally.
- Introduce AI/ML-based result interpretation without flagging the
  **EU AI Act** high-risk overlap: MDR/IVDR devices requiring Notified Body
  involvement are automatically high-risk under AI Act Art. 6(1) + Annex I,
  which brings its own documentation and human-oversight obligations.

## 11. Glossary

- **MDR** — Medical Device Regulation (EU) 2017/745
- **IVDR** — In Vitro Diagnostic Medical Devices Regulation (EU) 2017/746
- **MPDG** — Medizinprodukterecht-Durchführungsgesetz (German national
  implementing law)
- **PRRC** — Person Responsible for Regulatory Compliance
- **SaMD** — Software as a Medical Device
- **IFU** — Instructions For Use
- **UDI** — Unique Device Identification
- **EUDAMED** — European Database on Medical Devices
- **DiGA / DiGAV** — Digitale Gesundheitsanwendung / its ordinance
  (Germany's separate fast-track digital health app pathway, distinct from
  MDR/IVDR)
- **BfArM** — Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte (German
  competent authority)
- **PMS** — Post-Market Surveillance
- **PMCF** — Post-Market Clinical Follow-up
- **CAPA** — Corrective and Preventive Action
- **SOUP** — Software of Unknown Provenance (third-party/open-source
  dependencies in IEC 62304 terms)

## 12. Disclaimer (restated)

This file exists to reduce the chance that day-to-day code changes create
regulatory problems. It does **not** replace a qualified PRRC, a Notified
Body assessment, the Cube manufacturer's QMS, or legal/regulatory counsel.
When a change touches anything in this file's scope and the right answer
isn't obvious from the code alone, the correct action is to **ask a human**,
not to proceed on an assumption.
